  "articles": [
    {
      "title": "Three classes of consonants",
      "body": "<p>Thai has five tones: mid, low, falling, high and rising. Spelling shows which one a syllable has, but not with one mark per tone. Instead it combines three things: the class of the first consonant (middle, high or low), the tone mark if there is one, and whether the syllable ends in a vowel or a sound like m, n or ng (live), or in a short vowel or p, t, k (dead).</p>\n<p>That is why Thai keeps several letters for the same sound: <span lang=\"th\">ข</span> and <span lang=\"th\">ค</span> are both kh, but one is high and one is low, so <span lang=\"th\">ขา</span> (khǎa, leg) and <span lang=\"th\">คา</span> (khaa, to be stuck) have different tones.</p>\n<p>A good first step is to learn the nine middle-class letters by heart and the eleven high-class ones. Everything else is low.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Vowels go all around",
      "body": "<p>A Thai vowel can be written after the consonant (<span lang=\"th\">กา</span>), above it (<span lang=\"th\">กิ</span>), below it (<span lang=\"th\">กุ</span>), before it (<span lang=\"th\">เก</span>) or in several of those places at once (<span lang=\"th\">เกีย</span>). The consonant is always said first, so you read <span lang=\"th\">เก</span> as kee even though the vowel comes first on the page.</p>\n<p>A syllable with no vowel written between two consonants has a hidden short o or a: <span lang=\"th\">คน</span> is khon, person.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "No spaces between words",
      "body": "<p>Thai is written without spaces between words. A space marks the end of a phrase or sentence, a little like a comma or full stop. Readers find the word breaks from the shapes of syllables: a vowel that is written first, like <span lang=\"th\">เ</span> or <span lang=\"th\">ไ</span>, often starts a new syllable.</p>\n<p>At the end of a syllable many letters lose their own sound: only k, t, p, m, n, ng, w and y are heard. <span lang=\"th\">ท</span>, <span lang=\"th\">ศ</span> and <span lang=\"th\">ษ</span> all end in t, so <span lang=\"th\">บาท</span> (baht), <span lang=\"th\">อากาศ</span> (akat, weather) and <span lang=\"th\">พิเศษ</span> (phiset, special) all end the same way.</p>"
    }
  ]
